;;; `std.math.decimal-arith` — a malformed number: a name that is no digit, in a digit's
;;; place.
;;;
;;; ── What the theory promises ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A malformed numeral is `std.math.decimal`'s, flagged as it is written,
;;; wherever it sits, with no request reading it (Q-M1f.210): the loader
;;; states every fact nested in a fact the program writes, `(written T)`, and
;;; the module's rules read each place of each numeral off it (Q-M1f.272).
;;; The flag is `(not-decimal NUMERAL THING)`, on the smallest numeral at
;;; fault (Q-M1f.274), and `not-decimal-is-false` derives `(false)` from it.
;;; The request that holds the numeral is checked for none of it: no walk of
;;; `std.math.decimal-arith`'s reads a numeral's places for a fault, and the
;;; request's own flag, `(malformed REQUEST)`, is a digit's where a number goes.
;;;
;;; `numeral-digit`: the place of a digit holds `x`, which is none of the ten,
;;; in `(n (n z 1) x)`, inside the first operand. The rule has ten `neq` guards
;;; and no table: `neq` holds of every other name. The flag is `(not-decimal
;;; (n (n z 1) x) x)`, the smallest numeral that holds the letter.
;;;
;;; ── What the expectation means ─────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `(solve :expect (false))`: root is dead and R is empty, the one claim such
;;; a program can make. The sum beside it was computed and is in root's state,
;;; but a dead state is no answer. `ein solve` names the request, the fact the
;;; numeral was written in, in the unsat core, and `--trace` shows the flag
;;; and the contradiction as the first steps of root.

(import std.math.decimal-arith :symbols (decimal-arith add))
(import std.math.decimal :symbols (n))
(import std.math.arith :symbols (z))

(add (n z 2) (n z 2))
(add (n (n (n z 1) x) 7) (n z 5))

(solve :expect (false))
